{:edb/db-before-t 2842 :edb/db-after-t 2843 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536155 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:20:42.250Z" 13194139536155 true] [17592186166420 1100 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186166420 1100 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$f(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}$.\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b)$. Find $a$ given that $b=2$." 13194139536155 false] [17592186166420 1145 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}∫_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[\\frac{x^{2}y}{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 2y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{4}}{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea(D) &= ∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}∫_{0}^{2y}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = 2y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}2ydy \\\\\n&= [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2$. So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n64 &= f(a, 2) \\cdot 16 \\\\\n64 &= \\frac{a \\cdot 2}{2} \\cdot 16 \\\\\n64 &= 16a \\\\\na &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\left(4,2 \\right) \\in D\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $a = 4$." 13194139536155 false] [17592186166420 1145 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =\\frac{a\\cdot 2}{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =16a \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\left( 4,2 \\right) \\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a = 4.$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186166421 1100 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units." 13194139536155 true] [17592186166421 1100 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units." 13194139536155 false] [17592186166421 1145 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{ave} &= (1)/(Area(D))∬_(D)f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∬_(D)xydA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{x}^{3x}xydydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{xy^{2}}{2}]_{y = x}^{y = 3x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{-1}^{2}4x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[x^{4}]_{x =-1}^{x = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(16 - 1) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536155 false] [17592186166421 1145 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{3x}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=x}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{4}]_{x=-1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(16-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186166422 1100 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0$, and $y \\leq 0$." 13194139536155 false] [17592186166422 1100 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0,$ and $y \\leq 0.$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186166422 1145 "First, let's sketch our region $D$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3147/Source/Images/810ee225582208f31ff49948545b0349.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)15xy^{2}dA &= ∬_(Δ)15r\\cos θ \\cdot (r\\sin θ)^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}15r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ∫_{0}^{1}5r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ[r^{5}]_{0}^{1}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ(1^{5} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= [\\sin^{3} θ]_{3π/2}^{2π} \\\\\n&= (\\sin^{3} (2π) - \\sin^{3} (\\frac{3π}{2})) \\\\\n&= (0 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\n$$" 13194139536155 false] [17592186166422 1145 "First, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/a381fede820f9fc5ba08bb9569906d41a3e8f779de38b446c4f271ceef3a5ccd.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({1}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =({sin}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )-{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}.\n$$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186166815 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386350 13194139536155 true] [17592186166815 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386357 13194139536155 true] [17592186166815 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386364 13194139536155 true] [17592186166815 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386371 13194139536155 true] [17592186166816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386378 13194139536155 true] [17592186166816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386385 13194139536155 true] [17592186166816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386392 13194139536155 true] [17592186166816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386399 13194139536155 true] [17592186166817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386343 13194139536155 true] [17592186166817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386406 13194139536155 true] [17592186166817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386413 13194139536155 true] [17592186166817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386420 13194139536155 true] [17592186166817 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386427 13194139536155 true] [17592186167070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536155 true] [17592186167070 1100 "Consider the function $f(x, y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$f(x, y) = x^{2} + 2y + 1, D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$.\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a, b) ∈ D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z = f(x, y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a, b)$. Find $b$ given that $a = 1$." 13194139536155 false] [17592186167070 1100 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y+1,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186167070 1145 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}+y]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+12x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+12\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(1,b)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =({1}^{2}+2b+1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =12b+12 \\\\ 20 & =12b \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,\\frac{5}{3})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186167072 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536155 true] [17592186167072 1100 "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - 4y^{3}$ on the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$." 13194139536155 false] [17592186167072 1100 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186167072 1145 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y-{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-81)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{3}-81x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-81\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386343 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386344 13194139536155 true] [17592186386343 1089 "q-163070" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386343 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536155 true] [17592186386343 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." 13194139536155 true] [17592186386343 1113 #uuid "b77b3c65-9b31-5270-b685-33cd0e1cb660" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386343 1145 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡(0)-{sin}^{5}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386345 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386346 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386347 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386348 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386349 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1041 "selection" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386346 13194139536155 true] [17592186386344 1110 #uuid "c062c7cf-706d-5043-8a02-50c9729b03cb" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386345 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536155 true] [17592186386345 1111 #uuid "bbd8c209-7ef8-5554-b9a9-8a1d6606913f" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386346 1034 "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536155 true] [17592186386346 1111 #uuid "3c2f28dc-7652-5601-b7e3-58ce8a327f7e" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386347 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536155 true] [17592186386347 1111 #uuid "dd19b945-73c0-5450-af87-979bc3399c3e" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386348 1034 "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536155 true] [17592186386348 1111 #u