 that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x-y\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\frac{1}{3}{[{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139536155 true] [17592186386414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386415 13194139536155 true] [1